
[38;5;231m⏺[39m That settles it — [1marchitectural path[0m. Both answers point the same way, and they also mean the change as literally
  requested won't work:

  [2m▎[0m [3mIf no [38;5;153muserId[39m exists anywhere yet, nothing can pass one into [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. A parameter is a way to move a value you [0m
  [2m▎[0m [3malready have. You don't have one. So before any signature changes, we have to decide what establishes a user's [0m
  [2m▎[0m [3midentity and where login events get recorded.[0m

  I'll work through this as a design, one question at a time, and write a spec before any code.

  [1mFirst fork: what establishes the user's identity?[0m

  Right now [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub — it never contacts [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, it just returns [38;5;153m{success: true}[39m unconditionally. Nothing
  in the app can vouch that a user is who they claim. Whatever we pick here gets baked into every tracking record you
  ever store, so it's the expensive one to change later.

  - [1mAuth API assigns it[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m actually POSTs and the server returns a real [38;5;153muserId[39m. It's the only option where the ID
    means "this person authenticated." Costs: you need a real endpoint (or a defined mock contract), and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m
    becomes [38;5;153masync[39m, which changes its interface and its one caller. Cheap to build on later.
  - [1mClient-generated pseudonymous ID[0m — a UUID in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m. No backend needed, works today. But it identifies a
    [3mbrowser[0m, not a person: it resets when storage clears, splits across devices, and two people on one laptop share it.
    Expensive to undo — once these IDs are in your data, old events can't be re-attributed.
  - [1mUsername as the key[0m — zero new machinery, available right now at the call site. But usernames are typically mutable
    and are user-supplied data you may not want in an analytics store. If someone renames, their history splits in two.
    Re-keying stored events later is painful.

  [1mMy recommendation: auth API assigns it.[0m You said you want to track [3mwho[0m logged in. Only the server can answer that; the
  other two answer "which browser" or "what was typed in the box." The [38;5;153masync[39m change is real work, but retrofitting it
  after you've built tracking on a client-side ID is strictly worse than doing it now.

